- How would you modify for 32-bit inputs?
- Compare this to using built-ins like __builtin_ctzll in terms of portability and code size.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of bit-level data packing and word-level efficiency.

## Key Concepts
- Bit spreading (dilating)
- Inline masks and shifts for 32-bit results
- Morton codes for spatial indexing

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t part1by1(uint16_t n) {
  uint32_t x = n;
  x &= 0x0000FFFF;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t morton2D(uint16_t x, uint16_t y) {
  return (part1by1(y) << 1) | part1by1(x);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 3D Morton codes (x,y,z) and 64-bit result? 
- How would you adapt for non-power-of-two bit widths or streaming data?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128-bit arithmetic, two's-complement negation, and branchless masking, all crucial for low-level optimizations in high-performance systems.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit representation via hi/lo limbs
- Two's-complement negation across limb boundaries
- Branchless bit masking using bitwise operations
- Edge cases: x==0 and bit in hi vs lo limb

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 extractLowestSetBit128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t loNeg = (~x.lo) + 1;
  uint64_t hiNeg = (~x.hi) + (loNeg == 0);
  uint128 mask;
  mask.hi = x.hi & hiNeg;
  mask.lo = x.lo & loNeg;
  return mask;
}
```

## Examples
- Input: `{hi: 0, lo: 0b1000}` → Output: `{hi: 0, lo: 0b1000}`
- Input: `{hi: 0b1000, lo: 0}` → Output: `{hi: 0b1000, lo: 0}`

## Why This Is Asked

Demonstrates capability to manipulate multi-word integers with pure bit tricks, a common requirement in crypto and low-level systems programming. It tests cross-lane thinking and branch-free implementation.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-lane bit propagation between hi and lo.
- Branch-free 128-bit rotation using only shifts and OR.

## Code Example

```c
uint128 rotateLeft1281(uint128 x) {
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = (x.lo << 1) | (x.hi >> 63);
  r.hi = (x.hi << 1) | (x.lo >> 63);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to rotate left by k (0 <= k < 128) without branches?
- How would you test boundary cases like k=0, k=127, across hi/lo boundaries?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to compose 128-bit reversal from 64-bit primitives without branching.

## Key Concepts

- 64-bit bit reversal algorithm
- Cross-block mapping for 128-bit reversal
- Constant-time, no-branch implementation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch
uint128 reverseBits128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo_rev = rev64(x.lo);
  uint64_t hi_rev = rev64(x.hi);
  return (uint128){ .hi = lo_rev, .lo = hi_rev };
}
```

