- How does this scale if you had a 256-bit type?

## Why This Is Asked

In high-throughput diffing pipelines, diffusion-boosting byte-wise bit reversal can be done with a fixed, branchless sequence, testing mastery of multi-stage bit manipulations.

## Key Concepts

- Parallel bit-twiddling across 8-byte lanes
- Masks: 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F, 0x3333333333333333, 0x5555555555555555
- Branchless, constant-time operations

## Code Example

```javascript
function reverseBitsInBytes64(n) {
  n = ((n >>> 4n) | (n << 4n)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  n = ((n >>> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n) | ((n & 0x3333333333333333n) << 2n);
  n = ((n >>> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n) | ((n & 0x5555555555555555n) << 1n);
  return n;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test edge cases (0, all ones, alternating patterns)?
- How does endianness affect results across platforms?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to perform endianness reversal across a 128-bit value with no branches.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit layout via hi/lo; byte-level reversal; pure bit tricks.
- 64-bit byte swap implementation using shifts/masks.
- Branchless data movement across two halves.

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t bswap64(uint64_t x){
  x = ((x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32) | ((x & 0xFFFFFFFF00000000ULL) >> 32);
  x = ((x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((x & 0xFFFF0000FFFF0000ULL) >> 16);
  x = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8)  | ((x & 0xFF00FF00FF00FF00ULL) >> 8);
  return x;
}
void swapEndianness128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo){
  *out_hi = bswap64(lo);
  *out_lo = bswap64(hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to a 256-bit type? (Explain any performance considerations.)
- Could you implement a generic macro to swap endianness for any multiple of 8 bytes?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform cross-128-bit bit counting without loops, using portable bit-twiddling. Demonstrates knowledge of XOR for difference, and multi-word popcount without hardware intrinsics.

## Key Concepts

- XOR-based difference
- SWAR-style popcount on 64-bit words
- Combining counts for 128-bit data

## Code Example

```c
unsigned distance128(uint128 a, uint128 b) {
  uint64_t x = a.hi ^ b.hi;
  uint64_t y = a.lo ^ b.lo;
  // SWAR popcount on x
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  // SWAR popcount on y
  y = y - ((y >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  y = (y & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((y >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  y = (y + (y >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  y = y + (y >> 8);
  y = y + (y >> 16);
  y = y + (y >> 32);
  return (unsigned)(x & 0xFF) + (unsigned)(y & 0xFF);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to arbitrary n-word widths while maintaining no-loops constraints?
- Could you optimize using compiler intrinsics if available and fall back gracefully? If so, which intrinsics and how would you keep portability?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a robust, branchless 128-bit rotation, ensuring correct cross-boundary behavior and emphasis on manipulating split 64-bit halves.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit rotation across hi/lo halves
- Branchless implementation via masks and shifts
- Edge cases: n = 0, n = 64, n = 127

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n) {
  n %= 128;
  uint128 res;
  if (n == 0) return x; // illustrative intent; prefer mask-based approach to avoid branches
  // Branchless approach would compute both paths and select via masks
  if (n < 64) {
    res.lo = (x.lo << n) | (x.hi >> (64 - n));
    res.hi = (x.hi << n) | (x.lo >> (64 - n));
  } else {
    unsigned m = n - 64;
    res.lo = (x.hi << m) | (x.lo >> (64 - m));
    res.hi = (x.lo << m) | (x.hi >> (64 - m));
  }
  return res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test edge cases like n=0, n=64, n=127? 
- How would you adapt this for a 256-bit type?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests cross-word bit ops and branchless parity reduction. It exercises combining two 64-bit halves and a compact reduction, scalable to wider architectures.

## Key Concepts

- Parity via XOR folding
- 128-bit composition hi^lo
- Branchless reductions

## Code Example

```c
int parity128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  uint64_t x = hi ^ lo;
  x ^= x >> 32;
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  return x & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to a 256-bit value?  - Can you adapt this for streaming parity on large data chunks?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement a constant-time popcount-style operation using only masks and shifts, simulating a common bitmap diff in games or databases.

## Key Concepts
- Hamming weight via parallel reduction
- XOR to identify differences
- Mask sequences for bit counting

## Code Example
```cpp
int hammingDistance64(uint64 a, uint64 b){
  uint64 x = a ^ b;
  x = (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) + ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL) + ((x >> 4) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL);
  x = (x & 0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL) + ((x >> 8) & 0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL);
  x = (x & 0x0000ffff0000ffffULL) + ((x >> 16) & 0x0000ffff0000ffffULL);
  x = (x & 0x00000000ffffffffULL) + ((x >> 32) & 0x00000000ffffffffULL);
  return (int)x;
}
```

