## Why This Is Asked
Interleaving bits to form Morton codes is common in spatial indexing and GPU data layouts. The solution enforces branchless bit spreading using fixed masks, ensuring deterministic performance.

## Key Concepts
- Morton order (Z-order) bit interleaving
- Fixed-mask bit spreading (part1by1)
- Branchless implementation

## Code Example
```javascript
static inline uint64_t part1by1(uint32_t n) {
  uint64_t v = n;
  v = (v | (v << 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  v = (v | (v << 8))  & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  v = (v | (v << 4))  & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  v = (v | (v << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  v = (v | (v << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  return v;
}

uint64_t interleave32(uint32_t x, uint32_t y) {
  return (part1by1(y) << 1) | part1by1(x);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 64-bit inputs producing a 128-bit result?
- How does this interact with SIMD paths for batched data?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform fixed-bit manipulations without loops, using SWAR-style packing to reduce 64 bits to 32 bits in one pass.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR parallel bit extraction
- Fixed-mask chain for bit packing
- No branching for deterministic performance

## Code Example

```c
uint32 compressEvenBits64(uint64 x) {
    x = (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) | ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) | ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) | ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL);
    x = (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
    x = (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) | ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
    return (uint32)x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this to extract the odd bits instead of even bits?
- What are the trade-offs vs a loop-based approach on various architectures?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reduce a 128-bit value to a single parity bit using only bitwise ops, no branching.

## Key Concepts

- XOR-based parity reduction
- Cross word reduction
- Constant-time bit twiddling

## Code Example

```c
uint8_t parity128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo){
  uint64_t x = hi ^ lo;
  x ^= x >> 32;
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  x ^= x >> 2;
  x ^= x >> 1;
  return (uint8_t)(x & 1);
}
```

